WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.426 --> 00:00:11.428
So we want to integrate

00:00:11.428 --> 00:00:13.680
 the forest more into people's lives.

00:00:14.097 --> 00:00:16.016
You know, doing redneck stuff.

00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:19.019
If that helps, we'll do that.

00:00:20.103 --> 00:00:21.229
Hello, world.

00:00:21.229 --> 00:00:25.150
Over the years, I visited many Japanese
cities, towns, and villages.

00:00:25.316 --> 00:00:28.778
Places with millions of people
to places with a few hundred.

00:00:29.154 --> 00:00:31.740
This time, we're heading out 
to this tiny village,

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:34.034
up in the northern mountains
of Okayama Prefecture.

00:00:34.409 --> 00:00:36.911
Its name is Nishiawakura.

00:00:36.911 --> 00:00:40.665
It has just over 1300 people,
and is surrounded by thousands

00:00:40.665 --> 00:00:44.377
of hectares of manmade forests
and picturesque mountainsides.

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:48.465
And if you're thinking it's
like the last small town I covered, Kofu,

00:00:48.465 --> 00:00:51.301
I think you'll find
they’re very different places.

00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:53.511
You see, in the past decade or so, 

00:00:53.511 --> 00:00:57.015
Nishiawakura has become a famous
and award winning village

00:00:57.015 --> 00:01:00.060
within the sustainable
development community in Japan.

00:01:00.185 --> 00:01:03.772
By the way, Japanese governments
love the acronym SDG,

00:01:03.855 --> 00:01:06.816
which stands for 
“Sustainable Development Goals”

00:01:06.816 --> 00:01:10.195
Nishiawakura’s sustainable efforts
and openness to entrepreneurs

00:01:10.195 --> 00:01:13.406
has attracted over 200 people
from outside the village to immigrate.

00:01:13.782 --> 00:01:16.868
That's roughly 20% of the village population.

00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:18.620
If their efforts work,

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.080
and so far they seem to be, 

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.957
then the economic framework built in Nishiawakura

00:01:22.957 --> 00:01:25.752
may be applied to a variety
of other places in Japan 

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:27.462
that face similar problems, 

00:01:27.462 --> 00:01:32.175
Such as depopulation, job decline and environmental degradation.

00:01:32.217 --> 00:01:35.261
To see what was going
on, I had Bobby head out to Nishiawakura

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:39.349
several times over half a year
to film the villagers and their projects.

00:01:39.682 --> 00:01:41.976
In this video, we'll go over how the village

00:01:41.976 --> 00:01:44.104
since 2008, has carried out

00:01:44.104 --> 00:01:49.109
an ambitious multi-generation initiative called
“The 100 Year Vision of the Forest”

00:01:49.109 --> 00:01:51.694
With the aim of utilizing its natural resources

00:01:51.694 --> 00:01:55.115
to revitalize its economy
and become a self-sustaining village.

00:01:55.698 --> 00:01:58.785
To help paint a clearer picture
of what Nishiawakura is like

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:02.122
and why so many outsiders
are moving into the village every year.

00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:05.458
We'll meet a variety of villagers;
from foresters,

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:09.212
educators, entrepreneurs,
and leaders of the community.

00:02:09.212 --> 00:02:11.589
But first, let's orient ourselves a bit.

00:02:11.589 --> 00:02:13.216
Nishiawakura is up here.

00:02:13.216 --> 00:02:16.803
On the border between the prefectures
of Okayama and Tottori

00:02:17.137 --> 00:02:19.139
And depending on your definition of a city,

00:02:19.139 --> 00:02:22.809
the closest such places are
Tottori, Tsuyama, or Himeji.

00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:25.228
All about a 50 minute car ride away.

00:02:26.187 --> 00:02:29.023
Now, if you're like Bobby 
and visiting from Okayama city,

00:02:29.023 --> 00:02:31.109
the quickest route is to take the Super Inaba

00:02:31.109 --> 00:02:33.403
From Okayama station to Ohara station 

00:02:33.403 --> 00:02:34.904
And have a friend pick you up,

00:02:34.904 --> 00:02:37.866
because otherwise it's an hour long 
walk to Nishiawakura.

00:02:38.116 --> 00:02:42.412
But there is a cheaper local train line
that brings you to the heart of the village.

00:02:42.495 --> 00:02:45.707
However, it takes over
two hours from Okayama station.

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:50.128
On the plus side, it can be nice
to have an entire train car to yourself.

00:02:50.628 --> 00:02:52.380
What do you think, Bobby?

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:53.214
Indeed.

00:02:53.214 --> 00:02:56.217
And as Greg mentions,
the Super Inaba is really convenient.

00:02:56.593 --> 00:02:57.594
But the local train route 

00:02:57.594 --> 00:03:00.513
 definitely makes you feel like
you're going somewhere remote.

00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:03.683
It's not for everyone, but taking
the longer route every once in a while

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:07.770
can help build a sense of adventure,
so I recommend it if you're OK on time.

00:03:08.479 --> 00:03:10.732
Now, once I arrived at Nishiawakura station,

00:03:10.732 --> 00:03:13.443
it was just a two minute
walk to my base of operations.

00:03:13.443 --> 00:03:15.820
A bed and breakfast called 
Nokishita Toshokan

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:17.655
run by my friend, Hiromi Charles.

00:03:17.655 --> 00:03:21.201
They provide delicious homemade meals
as well as a relaxing atmosphere

00:03:21.201 --> 00:03:23.828
for you to truly kick back
during your time in the village.

00:03:23.828 --> 00:03:26.831
And of course, I interviewed Hiromi
about her important role in the village,

00:03:27.081 --> 00:03:29.209
as well as her decision
to move here from London.

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:31.336
But we'll cover that a bit later.

00:03:31.336 --> 00:03:34.631
First, we got to go into the manmade
forests of Nishiawakura

00:03:34.923 --> 00:03:38.343
because to truly wrap one's head
around the 100 year forest initiative

00:03:38.593 --> 00:03:40.511
and what all these entrepreneurs are doing,

00:03:40.511 --> 00:03:43.932
it's helpful to actually see what's
going on in these giant tree plantations.

00:03:44.057 --> 00:03:46.809
Now, with my poor sense of direction,
I would have gotten lost

00:03:46.809 --> 00:03:49.020
within the first ten minutes
of being in the forest.

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:52.815
So I was very fortunate to have Murooka-san,
our friendly neighborhood forester

00:03:52.815 --> 00:03:55.193
to guide me through the day's forest path.

00:04:01.491 --> 00:04:02.659
And he was right.

00:04:02.659 --> 00:04:04.244
I had no clue.

00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:07.163
You see, he wasn't
just introducing me to the forest.

00:04:07.163 --> 00:04:10.750
Murooka-san was at work,
marking a path for a future logging road

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:13.836
that foresters will use to transport
the felled trees back into town.

00:04:30.144 --> 00:04:33.147
It's not something that most people,
including myself, think about,

00:04:33.314 --> 00:04:36.859
but hauling big trees
out of a hilly forest is not an easy task.

00:04:36.985 --> 00:04:39.654
So creating safe and efficient
logging roads is essential

00:04:39.654 --> 00:04:41.155
for the forestry industry.

00:04:41.155 --> 00:04:44.701
Murooka-san and the forestry startup
he works for called Hyakumori, 

00:04:44.701 --> 00:04:46.411
uses data collected from the air via

00:04:46.411 --> 00:04:49.580
via the Geographic Information System 
or GIS,

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:53.084
which uses aircrafts with lasers
attached to them to accurately measure

00:04:53.084 --> 00:04:54.585
the dimensions of the forest.

00:04:54.585 --> 00:04:58.006
They use this lidar data, combined
with drone footage, to get relatively

00:04:58.006 --> 00:05:03.386
accurate measurements of tree size,
topography, and a sense of overall scale.

00:05:03.386 --> 00:05:05.680
Now, when it comes to evaluating
the quality of trees, 

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:09.100
generally speaking, the taller and straighter
the tree trunk, the better.

00:05:09.142 --> 00:05:11.686
You see the wood near
the top of the tree isn't really usable

00:05:11.686 --> 00:05:14.689
for architecture or carpentry
because it's so small.

00:05:14.814 --> 00:05:17.817
That's why long, straight and wide tree
trunks are so valuable.

00:05:18.234 --> 00:05:20.445
But also depending on where 
the trees are planted

00:05:20.445 --> 00:05:22.196
and how well they were taken care of,

00:05:22.238 --> 00:05:24.866
the trees can vary
quite a bit in size and shape,

00:05:24.866 --> 00:05:27.744
even though they're the same variety
and roughly the same age.

00:06:00.735 --> 00:06:03.905
Now, to avoid disputes among landowners,
after the trees are cut,

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:07.283
the foresters label each tree to identify
whose land it came from.

00:06:07.617 --> 00:06:10.244
Because, as you just saw,
some owners spend the time and money

00:06:10.244 --> 00:06:13.498
to send the forest and take care
of their trees while others don't.

00:06:14.082 --> 00:06:15.583
And as you can imagine, the owners

00:06:15.583 --> 00:06:18.586
who take care of the trees will produce
higher value lumber.

00:06:18.628 --> 00:06:21.297
Therefore, they earn more money
when the wood is sold.

00:06:21.297 --> 00:06:24.759
Currently, the village has signed contracts 
with over 650 landowners,

00:06:24.759 --> 00:06:27.762
covering 2500 hectares of forest.

00:06:27.762 --> 00:06:30.390
So that's quite the logistical hurdle,
as some landowners

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:33.184
don't live in the village
and some others have passed away.

00:06:33.184 --> 00:06:36.145
And tracking down
their family members can be difficult.

00:06:36.145 --> 00:06:38.689
And if you're wondering why a landowner
would ever want the village

00:06:38.689 --> 00:06:41.984
to manage their forest to begin with,
just think about how much time and effort

00:06:41.984 --> 00:06:43.820
is required to tend to a forest.

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:45.655
Through the 100 year initiative

00:06:45.655 --> 00:06:48.658
the village uses government funding to pay
for the thinning operations,

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:51.494
meaning that landowners
don't have to pay for the tending

00:06:51.494 --> 00:06:54.080
but still earn money from the trees
that are cut down.

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:56.958
So it's quite a convenient arrangement,
especially for those who aren't

00:06:56.958 --> 00:06:58.793
particularly interested in foresting

00:06:58.793 --> 00:07:01.212
or don't have time
to take care of their forests.

00:07:01.712 --> 00:07:05.383
So after our little trek in the woods
Murooka-san drove us back to his workplace,

00:07:05.383 --> 00:07:07.844
a forestry startup
company called Hyakumori,

00:07:07.844 --> 00:07:11.389
I got to meet their CEO, Tabata-san,
and he kindly explained to me

00:07:11.389 --> 00:07:13.933
the ins and outs of the forestry 
business within Nishiawakura.

00:07:15.143 --> 00:07:16.561
So I think it's really rooted in

00:07:16.561 --> 00:07:20.022
how Nishiawakura started
the 100 year forest initiative.

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:25.695
So Nishiawakura was supposed to be
merged into a big city.

00:07:25.736 --> 00:07:26.863
“Big city” right?

00:07:26.863 --> 00:07:28.489
“Big city” yeah.

00:07:28.489 --> 00:07:29.866
Mimasaka city.

00:07:29.866 --> 00:07:32.994
But Nishiawakura decided
they wanted to stay independent.

00:07:33.035 --> 00:07:36.914
So they had to find a way
to make the village sustainable.

00:07:36.914 --> 00:07:40.501
So they wanted to, you know, create
some kind of value within the village.

00:07:40.501 --> 00:07:45.339
So, you know, the need to create something
is really, I think, rooted in Nishiawakura.

00:07:45.465 --> 00:07:47.758
So as Tabata-san mentioned, in 2008,

00:07:47.758 --> 00:07:49.427
Nishiawakura was quite close to merging

00:07:49.427 --> 00:07:51.387
with the nearby city of Mimasaka,

00:07:51.387 --> 00:07:54.390
but in order to increase their odds
of survival as an independent village,

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:57.435
they brought in consultants
hired by the national government to assess

00:07:57.435 --> 00:08:00.438
what economic measures they could take
to help revitalize the village.

00:08:00.897 --> 00:08:04.484
This is where a man named Maki-san,
a consultant at the time, recommended

00:08:04.484 --> 00:08:08.988
Nishiawakura to try to utilize its surrounding
forest to establish a circular economy.

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:11.449
The village then used government 
grant money to build

00:08:11.449 --> 00:08:13.951
the necessary infrastructure, 
such as a sawmill,

00:08:13.951 --> 00:08:16.954
a lumber yard, and businesses
to manage their forests.

00:08:16.996 --> 00:08:20.833
The thing is, we want to create as much
value within the village as possible.

00:08:21.375 --> 00:08:25.296
So if you get closer to the final product,
there's more value added.

00:08:25.296 --> 00:08:26.130
Right?

00:08:26.130 --> 00:08:29.800
So it creates more employment 
within the village.

00:08:29.842 --> 00:08:32.428
So if we sell just timber,

00:08:32.428 --> 00:08:34.430
that's the end of the supply chain, right?

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:37.433
But if you include a sawmill, you get like,

00:08:37.433 --> 00:08:39.435
you know, ten people working the sawmill.

00:08:39.435 --> 00:08:41.896
And then if you get the timber
from the sawmill 

00:08:41.896 --> 00:08:43.731
and then put it into these floors,

00:08:43.731 --> 00:08:45.942
there's another ten people working for that.

00:08:45.942 --> 00:08:49.362
So, you know, getting closer to
the final product is really important.

00:08:50.029 --> 00:08:52.198
And it's not just forestry or lumber related jobs

00:08:52.198 --> 00:08:54.534
that have contributed
to the economy in the village.

00:08:54.617 --> 00:08:58.913
As of 2008, over 50 new businesses
have sprung up within Nishiawakura.

00:08:58.913 --> 00:09:01.999
All started and owned by residents
specializing in various fields.

00:09:02.583 --> 00:09:05.920
We wanted to visit them all, but that would have been a logistical nightmare

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:07.922
So we decided on visiting a select few.

00:09:07.922 --> 00:09:10.800
Including one of the village's oldest
startups, A0.

00:09:11.217 --> 00:09:14.762
To put it simply, A0
is a multidisciplinary consulting company

00:09:14.762 --> 00:09:18.766
that's involved with farming, dining,
carpentry and a variety of other services.

00:09:19.141 --> 00:09:21.936
And we got to interview
one of the core members, Nishioka-san.

00:09:21.936 --> 00:09:23.688
He is one of many ambitious outsiders

00:09:23.688 --> 00:09:27.024
who moved to Nishiawakura to start a new life
with his young family.

00:10:33.633 --> 00:10:35.301
We'll come back to the A0 group in a bit

00:10:35.301 --> 00:10:37.678
to cover some of their other
innovative businesses.

00:10:37.678 --> 00:10:39.347
But first let's try to connect the dots

00:10:39.347 --> 00:10:41.390
with what Tabata-san and 
Nishioka-san have said.

00:10:41.682 --> 00:10:45.436
So despite the abundance of cypress and cedar trees in the village, there is a limit

00:10:45.436 --> 00:10:48.439
to how many can be cut and managed
within a certain time period.

00:10:48.522 --> 00:10:49.815
This could be due to landowner

00:10:49.815 --> 00:10:52.818
disputes, environmental concerns
or even funding issues.

00:10:53.110 --> 00:10:56.447
They can't just chop down as many trees
as they want and expect to make a profit.

00:10:56.864 --> 00:10:58.699
Therefore, it's
vital for the village to produce

00:10:58.699 --> 00:11:02.286
and sell the highest value wood products
such as furniture or architecture,

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:03.996
to get a high return on investment,

00:11:03.996 --> 00:11:06.666
while also considering
the environmental effects on the village.

00:11:07.625 --> 00:11:09.043
Now, something we haven't addressed yet

00:11:09.043 --> 00:11:12.296
is how much of the landscape in
 Nishiawakura and much of Japan,

00:11:12.296 --> 00:11:14.548
became so packed with manmade forests,

00:11:14.548 --> 00:11:17.551
and here we have Tabata-san
to give us a quick history lesson.

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:22.848
In Japan, most of the cedar and cypress trees
and most other forests were cut down

00:11:22.848 --> 00:11:26.977
during World War II,
and many houses were burnt down.

00:11:27.269 --> 00:11:30.272
So there was a national initiative
to grow trees,

00:11:30.856 --> 00:11:33.943
most of them to be used in houses.

00:11:33.943 --> 00:11:35.820
You know, during that time.

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:38.072
Cedar and cypress trees were very valuable.

00:11:38.072 --> 00:11:40.449
So if you cut down like five trees,

00:11:40.449 --> 00:11:43.452
you could get the money to go to college
or get a car.

00:11:43.869 --> 00:11:44.870
Five trees?

00:11:44.870 --> 00:11:45.454
Yeah.

00:11:45.454 --> 00:11:48.791
You know, it was that valuable
 to have some trees.

00:11:49.417 --> 00:11:54.004
So people, you know, really wanted to plant trees 
for their grandson, grandkids,

00:11:54.296 --> 00:11:57.800
It turns out, it takes like 60
or 70 years to grow a tree.

00:11:58.467 --> 00:12:02.138
So during those 60-70 years,
many things changed.

00:12:02.471 --> 00:12:05.933
One of them was that, 
trees started being imported

00:12:05.933 --> 00:12:09.186
from Canada and Russia
and many other places.

00:12:09.478 --> 00:12:12.690
So, you know, the value went down
and also people

00:12:12.690 --> 00:12:15.693
stopped making houses
with, you know, timber.

00:12:15.943 --> 00:12:16.861
Oh, okay. Yeah.

00:12:16.861 --> 00:12:19.363
You know, it's mostly concrete
and things like that.

00:12:19.363 --> 00:12:22.533
So the value of, timber really went down

00:12:22.825 --> 00:12:25.202
1/20 of what it used to be.

00:12:25.202 --> 00:12:29.999
It costs pretty much $25,000
to plant trees in one hectare

00:12:29.999 --> 00:12:33.210
and then grow them to be 60 years old.

00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:36.297
And the same amount of, trees
that would be grown

00:12:36.297 --> 00:12:39.508
 would be sold for about $10,000.

00:12:39.508 --> 00:12:44.722
So, you know, it's planting and then also
protecting the trees from animals.

00:12:45.097 --> 00:12:47.433
And then you also have to weed the area.

00:12:47.433 --> 00:12:50.811
So that the trees grow larger
and then you have to do

00:12:50.853 --> 00:12:54.732
thinning activities,
and also pruning of the branches

00:12:54.732 --> 00:12:58.110
to make sure that they make good timber.

00:12:58.110 --> 00:12:58.694
Yeah.

00:12:58.694 --> 00:13:03.949
So in Japan we plant 
about 3,000 saplings per hectare.

00:13:03.949 --> 00:13:08.579
And then we have to thin them to be about
500 or 700 per hectare.

00:13:08.704 --> 00:13:11.582
So you have to thin them a lot.

00:13:12.208 --> 00:13:15.211
Now, in a previous
video I made about trees in Japan,

00:13:15.336 --> 00:13:19.048
I briefly talked about forest thinning
and its environmental effects.

00:13:19.590 --> 00:13:22.676
To recap,
thinning is the selective removal of trees

00:13:22.676 --> 00:13:25.679
to improve the overall health
and growth of a forest.

00:13:25.846 --> 00:13:28.933
By reducing overcrowding,
the remaining trees have better access

00:13:28.933 --> 00:13:33.020
to sunlight, water, and nutrients,
promoting stronger, more resilient growth.

00:13:33.479 --> 00:13:36.482
This also has a positive effect
on biodiversity

00:13:36.482 --> 00:13:39.944
and in theory can provide
more CO2 absorption in the long run.

00:13:40.486 --> 00:13:44.573
But thinning is expensive and
many landowners don't want to pay for it.

00:13:44.865 --> 00:13:46.784
There is a number of reasons for this.

00:13:46.784 --> 00:13:50.913
One is that the trees being cut down
may not be high value lumber.

00:13:50.913 --> 00:13:54.375
i.e., long, straight, and wide trees.

00:13:54.375 --> 00:13:55.709
Well, you may ask, 

00:13:55.709 --> 00:13:58.420
“Why not just sell the wood
 as woodchips for fuel?”

00:13:58.838 --> 00:14:01.924
Unfortunately,
the market price of woodchips is so low,

00:14:02.132 --> 00:14:05.511
mostly due to cheaply imported wood,
that the cost of transporting

00:14:05.511 --> 00:14:08.514
the wood outweighs
the revenue brought in by the woodchips.

00:14:08.973 --> 00:14:12.810
And that begs the question
why is transporting the wood so expensive?

00:14:13.227 --> 00:14:16.021
Well, logging in forestry requires
trained experts

00:14:16.021 --> 00:14:19.149
to safely and precisely
cut down sections of the forest,

00:14:19.483 --> 00:14:23.320
as we saw earlier with Murooka-san,
for efficient thinning operations,

00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:26.740
logging roads have to be constructed,
which requires money.

00:14:27.199 --> 00:14:29.285
Then there's the use
of big specialized machinery

00:14:29.285 --> 00:14:32.288
that comes with operation
fuel and maintenance costs.

00:14:32.454 --> 00:14:36.125
So when Tabata-san
says that it takes about $25,000 to grow

00:14:36.125 --> 00:14:40.713
one hectare of forest, but they only make
10,000 back, you can start to see why.

00:14:41.088 --> 00:14:44.550
In Japan, thinning forests
generally doesn't make economic sense.

00:14:45.009 --> 00:14:48.053
An unofficial estimate of 
30-40% of felled trees

00:14:48.053 --> 00:14:51.307
are left in the forest
to rot, due to high transportation costs.

00:14:51.932 --> 00:14:54.435
There's very little
published data on this, but it's kind of

00:14:54.435 --> 00:14:58.147
an open secret among landowners,
foresters and government officials.

00:14:58.981 --> 00:15:01.233
The point is, in many parts of the country,

00:15:01.233 --> 00:15:04.236
there is a lot of unutilized lumber
that's just left to rot in the forest after thinning,

00:15:04.236 --> 00:15:08.699
which means more CO2
gets released back into the atmosphere.

00:15:08.908 --> 00:15:12.036
So this poses both economic
and environmental challenges.

00:15:12.578 --> 00:15:16.415
Maintaining forests, require,
you know, a lot of things.

00:15:16.415 --> 00:15:21.170
One of them is to make sure
that the wood is really used

00:15:21.170 --> 00:15:23.339
in an ecological way.

00:15:23.339 --> 00:15:27.760
Using timber instead of fossil fuel
 is probably useful.

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:30.763
So a lot of people agree
that, you know, it's a good thing

00:15:30.763 --> 00:15:34.099
Cutting down trees itself
costs some carbon footprint.

00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:38.646
But if you really use it for like 
these kind of floors and houses,

00:15:38.812 --> 00:15:44.318
you could keep the carbon there,
 within those kind of structures.

00:15:45.319 --> 00:15:46.695
And when it comes to clearcutting,

00:15:46.695 --> 00:15:48.948
which is the harvesting of 
large sections of forest,

00:15:48.948 --> 00:15:53.452
typically only about 20% of felled trees 
are sold as high quality lumber, 

00:15:53.452 --> 00:15:56.455
with about 50%
being sold for use as plywood.

00:15:56.622 --> 00:16:00.626
The remaining 30% of low quality
wood has little to no commercial value.

00:16:01.210 --> 00:16:04.588
So in Nishiawakura,
to make the most of its natural resources,

00:16:04.713 --> 00:16:08.092
the low quality wood
is cut into wood chips and used as biomass

00:16:08.092 --> 00:16:11.261
to heat the elementary school,
junior high school, kindergarten,

00:16:11.553 --> 00:16:14.473
senior care facilities,
as well as the village office, slash

00:16:14.473 --> 00:16:17.977
library, slash recreation center,
which was recently rebuilt

00:16:17.977 --> 00:16:22.564
and has a centralized HVAC system
that makes use of large biomass boilers.

00:16:23.482 --> 00:16:26.777
About 1,000 tons of wood waste is utilized
per year and provides

00:16:26.777 --> 00:16:30.155
about 40% of the heating
needs for all public buildings.

00:16:30.781 --> 00:16:34.159
That means almost half of the public
infrastructure is heated through locally

00:16:34.159 --> 00:16:36.203
supplied energy resources.

00:16:36.203 --> 00:16:40.290
Eventually, the village wants to no longer
depend on any imported fossil fuels,

00:16:40.457 --> 00:16:43.544
which during times of war,
tariffs and trade disagreements

00:16:43.836 --> 00:16:46.839
can bring small towns
and cities to their knees.

00:16:47.047 --> 00:16:50.634
On a side note, the cooling system
at the village hall is also really neat.

00:16:51.051 --> 00:16:54.972
You see, there's an underground
well with naturally cold running water,

00:16:54.972 --> 00:16:58.726
so they use a solar powered
ventilation system to push the cooler

00:16:58.726 --> 00:17:00.769
underground air up toward the building

00:17:00.769 --> 00:17:03.939
through these tiny circular
air ducts installed throughout the floors.

00:17:04.815 --> 00:17:07.276
Anyway,
while we're on the topic of the village hall

00:17:07.276 --> 00:17:09.695
Bobby got to interview the village mayor.

00:17:09.695 --> 00:17:10.779
Mayor Aoki.

00:17:47.733 --> 00:17:52.071
The mayor's belief in self-sufficiency for both energy and food really resonated with me.

00:17:52.071 --> 00:17:55.866
However, one snag here is that the biomass boilers that provide much of

00:17:55.866 --> 00:17:59.912
the heating energy for Nishiawakura are not made in the village or even in Japan.

00:18:00.287 --> 00:18:02.956
They're made in Italy,
so they're not only expensive

00:18:02.956 --> 00:18:05.959
due to exchange
rates, shipping costs and tariffs,

00:18:05.959 --> 00:18:09.838
but maintenance can also be pricey
and quite frankly, a pain to deal with.

00:18:33.570 --> 00:18:35.906
As you can see, the mayor is quite outspoken

00:18:35.906 --> 00:18:37.783
and we'll get to hear more from him later.

00:18:37.908 --> 00:18:40.077
So far, we've met foresters
and those involved

00:18:40.077 --> 00:18:43.330
with setting up the new economic
infrastructure of Nishiawakura.

00:18:43.330 --> 00:18:45.874
But let's hear from someone
within the architectural world

00:18:45.874 --> 00:18:48.710
who helps increase the economic value
of the local lumber.

00:18:48.710 --> 00:18:50.546
Meet, Naoko-san, co-founder

00:18:50.546 --> 00:18:54.716
of one of Nishiawakura’s most successful
architectural firms called Youbi.

00:18:54.758 --> 00:18:57.261
Something I wanted to ask Naoko-san was
was why carpentry

00:18:57.261 --> 00:19:00.889
and architecture industries
hadn't sprung up in Nishiawakura earlier,

00:19:00.889 --> 00:19:02.391
even though the area had good weather

00:19:02.391 --> 00:19:05.352
and plenty of cypress and cedar trees
to use as the raw materials.

00:20:11.501 --> 00:20:14.338
I think many of us who live in
Japan share the experience of asking

00:20:14.338 --> 00:20:17.382
why something is done a certain way
and being given the response,

00:20:17.758 --> 00:20:20.302
“Because that's the way it is,
or always has been”

00:20:20.302 --> 00:20:23.305
which is a great belief when you don't
want people to rock the boat,

00:20:23.388 --> 00:20:26.600
but when the ship is sinking, like
in the case of many struggling villages,

00:20:26.975 --> 00:20:28.310
such a traditional mindset

00:20:28.310 --> 00:20:31.521
might hinder a community's ability
to face modern challenges.

00:20:31.813 --> 00:20:33.649
So we'll hear once again from the mayor

00:20:33.649 --> 00:20:36.693
about his thoughts on the importance
of adaptability for survival.

00:21:45.595 --> 00:21:49.224
This flexibility that the mayor describes
and the village’s support of new ventures

00:21:49.224 --> 00:21:52.227
really makes Nishiawakura an 
entrepreneur’s ideal village

00:21:52.602 --> 00:21:55.188
Now so far we’ve focused a lot about the forestry industry

00:21:55.188 --> 00:21:58.317
because it's the biggest part
of Nishiawakura's economy and also because

00:21:58.317 --> 00:22:01.361
it's so complex from an environmental
and economic standpoint,

00:22:01.486 --> 00:22:03.488
I honestly felt like I was writing
a college thesis

00:22:03.488 --> 00:22:05.198
or researching for this video,

00:22:05.198 --> 00:22:07.909
but I want to assure you that there's
quite a bit of economic diversity

00:22:07.909 --> 00:22:08.618
within the village,

00:22:08.618 --> 00:22:13.206
such as a cage free chicken farmer slash
consultant slash designer named Hada-san.

00:22:55.832 --> 00:22:59.169
Hada-san’s chickens arrived a few weeks
after my visit, but they seem to be doing

00:22:59.169 --> 00:23:02.214
quite well, so I wish him and his team
continued success.

00:23:02.672 --> 00:23:05.384
At A0, the multifaceted consulting firm,

00:23:05.384 --> 00:23:08.804
they also sell animal products
such as venison and farm raised eels.

00:23:08.804 --> 00:23:10.430
But with environmental, community

00:23:10.430 --> 00:23:13.433
and economic sustainability
in the forefront of their business model.

00:24:07.070 --> 00:24:09.781
So I think it's about time to head back
to Nokishita Toshokan,

00:24:09.781 --> 00:24:12.951
the bed and breakfast I stayed at
and hear a bit from my friend Hiromi.

00:24:13.243 --> 00:24:17.789
So I live here in Nishiawakura,
because this is where my father is from.

00:24:18.165 --> 00:24:22.127
I sort of inherited this house
then because my grandma,

00:24:22.294 --> 00:24:26.506
when she was alive and she loved reading,
she loved books.

00:24:26.506 --> 00:24:31.052
And so from the mid 1960s, for 10 years

00:24:31.052 --> 00:24:34.097
she opened up this room to local kids

00:24:34.097 --> 00:24:37.100
when we didn't have
a public library in the village,

00:24:37.601 --> 00:24:41.813
since she paid for my education
abroad, basically.

00:24:42.481 --> 00:24:44.983
And, I wanted to do something

00:24:44.983 --> 00:24:47.861
to remember her and to show my gratitude.

00:24:49.279 --> 00:24:51.323
Another popular place to stay in Nishiawakura

00:24:51.323 --> 00:24:54.701
is an onsen slash cafe
slash guesthouse called Motoyu

00:24:55.243 --> 00:24:58.371
Many locals come here to bathe
in the revitalizing hot spring waters.

00:24:58.371 --> 00:25:01.666
After a long day's work
and out-of-town visitors staying overnight

00:25:01.708 --> 00:25:05.504
can enjoy unique experiences like a DIY
sauna that looks like a giant egg.

00:25:05.712 --> 00:25:07.672
Right outside the entrance of the building,

00:25:07.672 --> 00:25:09.382
There's an impressive wood
furnace out back

00:25:09.382 --> 00:25:11.801
that helps heat up
the onsen temperatures just right.

00:25:11.801 --> 00:25:15.013
And you guessed it, they use wood
from the surrounding forest.

00:25:15.013 --> 00:25:17.974
They also serve locally produced meat
like venison or boar,

00:25:17.974 --> 00:25:19.392
which is quite delicious

00:25:19.392 --> 00:25:22.771
And fun fact the owner of Motoyu is a 
former competitive wrestler

00:25:22.979 --> 00:25:26.316
and also owns one of the most successful
wrestling uniform companies in Japan.

00:25:26.650 --> 00:25:29.653
So again, many hats, lots of ambitions.

00:25:30.445 --> 00:25:34.241
But let’s say you don't want to work in forestry,
agriculture, or hospitality industries.

00:25:34.574 --> 00:25:37.494
What other options are there for people
who want to live in the village?

00:25:37.494 --> 00:25:39.079
I asked Naoko-san, who’s involved with

00:25:39.079 --> 00:25:42.499
with various entrepreneurs and town
initiatives, her thoughts.

00:26:26.418 --> 00:26:29.379
I didn't get to visit the elementary
or middle school, but I was fortunate

00:26:29.379 --> 00:26:32.591
enough to visit the Montessori school run
by another out-of-town resident.

00:26:32.757 --> 00:26:34.259
Mayuko-san.

00:26:34.259 --> 00:26:38.471
Our family moved to the village
of Nishiawakura seven years ago,

00:26:39.139 --> 00:26:43.226
and right now I offer
a Montessori classroom environment

00:26:43.643 --> 00:26:47.272
for children from two and a half years
old, up to six years old.

00:26:47.731 --> 00:26:51.443
I offer classes
so that they can come just once a week

00:26:51.860 --> 00:26:54.863
to experience that environment.

00:26:54.863 --> 00:26:56.823
Those people who choose

00:26:56.823 --> 00:27:00.327
to move to Nishiawakura,
they are interested in entrepreneurship.

00:27:00.535 --> 00:27:04.122
They also want their children
to choose their life

00:27:04.581 --> 00:27:07.125
their career and Montessori in a way,

00:27:07.125 --> 00:27:11.630
we let them practice
making their own choices.

00:27:12.589 --> 00:27:13.757
And behind the village hall,

00:27:13.757 --> 00:27:17.469
there's an after school recreation center
called Pocket that just opened last year.

00:27:17.927 --> 00:27:21.681
It acts as a type of third space for young
students to visit, learn and have fun.

00:27:22.098 --> 00:27:25.101
And we got a VIP tour
from their charming hosts

00:33:41.769 --> 00:33:43.146
There's also a kitchen at Pocket

00:33:43.146 --> 00:33:46.107
where kids can decide on recipes
and cook for themselves, too

00:33:46.107 --> 00:33:48.818
These two chefs made us some candied
mandarin oranges

00:33:48.818 --> 00:33:51.821
that would melt your heart
and break your teeth.

00:33:53.322 --> 00:33:56.534
It was very generous of Sarina and her
little helper to guide us through Pocket.

00:33:56.951 --> 00:33:59.996
I wish every village
had such a cool rec center for kids.

00:33:59.996 --> 00:34:03.207
Now it was great to catch a glimpse
of Montessori and Pocket,

00:34:03.207 --> 00:34:04.751
but I still wanted to get a teenager's

00:34:04.751 --> 00:34:07.962
perspective on the village's education
and overall qualities.

00:34:07.962 --> 00:34:10.631
And that's where my young friend
Hiro comes in.

00:34:10.631 --> 00:34:14.886
I'm currently
a third year student in high school.

00:34:14.886 --> 00:34:17.013
I go to school by train.

00:34:17.013 --> 00:34:22.060
It takes about an hour and a half, and
then I bike to school for like 15 minutes.

00:34:22.518 --> 00:34:26.689
Most of the high school students
who grew up in Nishiawakura,

00:34:26.939 --> 00:34:31.486
they don't live here because
the nearest high school will be in Tsuyama.

00:34:31.694 --> 00:34:34.572
It's about like, one hour away by car.

00:34:34.572 --> 00:34:37.575
Because if you go to Tottori like me,

00:34:37.658 --> 00:34:41.120
we have a train
so I can come back every day.

00:34:41.370 --> 00:34:46.000
But in Tsuyama, we don't have,
like a good train to get on.

00:34:46.042 --> 00:34:50.505
So they have to stay in a dormitory
and they don't come back here that often.

00:34:50.838 --> 00:34:55.718
So the problem
now of Nishiawakura is the young generation,

00:34:55.718 --> 00:35:01.891
they go out of this village before
they understand the beauty of the village.

00:35:01.974 --> 00:35:04.102
What's unique about the village.

00:35:04.102 --> 00:35:08.147
So they won't come back when they get older.

00:35:08.147 --> 00:35:12.026
They just go to,
you know, big cities like Tokyo.

00:35:12.026 --> 00:35:15.029
Because I don't meet any of my friends,

00:35:15.071 --> 00:35:18.324
like my friends in junior high school,

00:35:18.908 --> 00:35:21.327
or like primary school now.

00:35:21.327 --> 00:35:23.079
And now I’m a high school student

00:35:23.079 --> 00:35:26.666
so I understand more
how this revitalization

00:35:26.666 --> 00:35:29.669
thing that Nishiawakura is trying to do.

00:35:29.669 --> 00:35:31.045
So I try to, you know,

00:35:32.380 --> 00:35:33.923
go to events.

00:35:33.923 --> 00:35:39.095
I, exhibited my artwork
in this forest festival held in Nishiawakura.

00:35:39.428 --> 00:35:42.890
So I try to engage more in the village.

00:35:43.266 --> 00:35:47.895
But you can't do that
when you live in Tsuyama.

00:35:47.895 --> 00:35:53.526
So it's hard to, you know, notice
small things in the village.

00:35:54.360 --> 00:35:57.363
So even though the village
is full of friendly people surrounded

00:35:57.363 --> 00:36:01.409
by beautiful nature and hosts
a number of fun events, a core problem

00:36:01.659 --> 00:36:04.287
is that teenagers are often missing
from the equation

00:36:04.287 --> 00:36:07.498
due to a lack of transportation
and education options.

00:36:07.790 --> 00:36:10.793
This will be a big challenge for
the village community to try and solve,

00:36:10.960 --> 00:36:14.797
but as long as new people come in and
offer new solutions, there's always hope.

00:36:15.006 --> 00:36:18.134
And this is where we'll introduce
another key resource that Nishiawakura

00:36:18.134 --> 00:36:21.721
utilizes as well,
the Chiiki Okoshi Kyoryokutai

00:36:21.971 --> 00:36:24.432
In English,
the organization roughly translates

00:36:24.432 --> 00:36:27.977
to local revitalization support
or support team,

00:36:28.394 --> 00:36:32.565
and nearly 1,100 villages, towns, and cities
take part in the program,

00:36:32.773 --> 00:36:36.277
which is funded by the Ministry
of Internal Affairs and Communications.

00:36:36.777 --> 00:36:39.322
Nishiawakura is considered
one of the most desirable

00:36:39.322 --> 00:36:42.366
and highly rated places
to work as a Kyoryokutai member.

00:36:42.909 --> 00:36:46.537
Currently, there are over 50 Kyoryokutai
members working in the village

00:36:46.537 --> 00:36:49.540
for various businesses,
some even starting their own.

00:36:49.999 --> 00:36:52.960
To get a better idea of what they do
and how it's going,

00:36:52.960 --> 00:36:56.714
Bobby interviewed two Kyoryokutai members
who work at Hyakumori.

00:36:57.465 --> 00:37:00.134
Well, like, the main objective of my rural

00:37:00.134 --> 00:37:03.304
promotion position
is to bring more people to the forest.

00:37:03.429 --> 00:37:07.808
Right now we're focusing mainly
on like activities that people are

00:37:07.934 --> 00:37:13.272
pretty familiar with, like trail running,
mountain biking and hiking and walking.

00:37:13.564 --> 00:37:16.234
I want to kind of explore more,

00:37:16.234 --> 00:37:19.237
but for example,
we just had like a forest festival.

00:37:19.237 --> 00:37:23.532
And there I,
invited my dance friend from Osaka

00:37:23.532 --> 00:37:27.828
and she did a dance improvization
workshop, kind of like

00:37:28.329 --> 00:37:32.166
trying to diversify the type of activities

00:37:32.166 --> 00:37:35.086
that people can enjoy while
out in the forest. Yeah.

00:38:17.295 --> 00:38:20.214
So, you know when people come to Nishiawakura,

00:38:20.214 --> 00:38:24.427
they usually go to the onsen,
but there's not much else to do.

00:38:24.969 --> 00:38:26.345
So we want to, you know,

00:38:26.345 --> 00:38:29.557
provide them with some kind of activity
to do within the village.

00:38:30.182 --> 00:38:32.977
I saw the poster at the library
for laser tag.

00:38:32.977 --> 00:38:33.311
Yeah.

00:38:33.311 --> 00:38:36.105
And it just reminded me
of like, a redneck family in America.

00:38:38.107 --> 00:38:38.733
I mean, like, you

00:38:38.733 --> 00:38:42.695
know, doing fun stuff in the forest,
but we want to make sure that

00:38:42.695 --> 00:38:46.824
people actually, you know, feel
that it's their forest as well.

00:38:46.824 --> 00:38:50.953
It's kind of like secluded from other,
you know, ways of life within the village

00:38:50.953 --> 00:38:51.787
right now.

00:38:51.787 --> 00:38:55.791
So we want to integrate the forest
more into people's lives,

00:38:56.459 --> 00:38:58.377
you know, doing redneck stuff.

00:38:58.377 --> 00:39:00.171
If that helps, we'll do that.

00:39:02.798 --> 00:39:07.803
We visited Nishiawakura three times,
and the first two times

00:39:07.803 --> 00:39:10.806
we were really just like getting feelers
for the village.

00:39:11.057 --> 00:39:14.643
After the second visit,
Taba-chan decided to apply

00:39:14.643 --> 00:39:17.855
for a Chiiki Okoshi
like rural promotion position.

00:39:17.855 --> 00:39:21.650
Once that got accepted, that's
when we knew like there was actually a job

00:39:22.234 --> 00:39:26.072
and then the second necessity
was, a place to live.

00:39:26.155 --> 00:39:29.825
So we just happened to get lucky because,
Taba-chan

00:39:29.825 --> 00:39:33.371
found a house for us
that was, available for rent.

00:39:33.662 --> 00:39:35.706
Yeah. Without, like, Taba-chan

00:39:35.706 --> 00:39:39.543
Like, without him being here
and having local connections.

00:39:39.543 --> 00:39:42.004
I don't think, like,
the move would have been that smooth.

00:39:43.214 --> 00:39:45.841
And in case you were curious, Minami
and Moe are expected

00:39:45.841 --> 00:39:50.388
to be hired full time at Hyakumori after their
Kyoryokutai grant period is finished.

00:39:50.679 --> 00:39:53.474
So whether it's to Kyoryokutai
or new entrepreneurs,

00:39:53.474 --> 00:39:56.977
the village is getting a much
needed influx of fresh ideas and manpower.

00:39:57.520 --> 00:40:00.523
But even though incoming residents
are a great thing for Nishiawakura,

00:40:00.606 --> 00:40:03.859
I couldn't help but wonder about
the forecast of the village's population.

00:40:04.360 --> 00:40:06.404
If we compare data from ten years ago,

00:40:06.404 --> 00:40:09.365
the village has been shrinking
at about 1.4% a year.

00:40:09.573 --> 00:40:13.160
It's way better than the national average,
which is about 2.47% a year.

00:40:13.619 --> 00:40:17.081
And this may be an irrelevant comparison,
but a nearby former village called

00:40:17.081 --> 00:40:21.043
Higashiawakura decided to merge 
with Mimasaka city, back in 2008.

00:40:21.544 --> 00:40:24.755
Over the past ten years, 
Higashiawakura’s population has fallen

00:40:24.755 --> 00:40:28.300
from 1189 people to 908 people

00:40:28.300 --> 00:40:30.761
an annual loss of 2.6%.

00:40:30.970 --> 00:40:33.973
They also no longer have a kindergarten
or elementary school,

00:40:33.973 --> 00:40:36.725
which makes it difficult
to appeal to young families.

00:40:36.725 --> 00:40:39.603
So with all that said,
I wanted to hear from inbound residents

00:40:39.603 --> 00:40:44.316
who'd already had children before moving to the village about raising kids in Nishiawakura.

00:40:44.316 --> 00:40:45.359
Before moving here

00:40:45.359 --> 00:40:49.697
the thing we were concerned the most about 
was whether or not our older daughter

00:40:50.156 --> 00:40:53.159
could fit in this rural,

00:40:53.159 --> 00:40:56.579
traditional Japanese school culture.

00:40:56.871 --> 00:41:01.542
But the fact was that
people were so kind and welcoming.

00:41:01.959 --> 00:41:05.588
And then what we were surprised
the most was there

00:41:05.629 --> 00:41:08.632
just so many people fluent in English.

00:41:09.133 --> 00:41:13.137
So we just felt totally at home and welcome

00:41:13.888 --> 00:41:14.763
So yes.

00:41:14.763 --> 00:41:18.809
So I do get my guests who are thinking
about moving to the village,

00:41:19.059 --> 00:41:23.147
and I try to be really honest about it,
but I always say, well,

00:41:23.147 --> 00:41:26.442
I just share my experience about my kids
growing up here.

00:41:26.484 --> 00:41:31.489
And, you know, the neighbors are really
happy to see kids running around.

00:41:31.655 --> 00:41:35.409
They don't complain, generally speaking,
about the noise that they make,

00:41:36.160 --> 00:41:37.286
and they're really welcome.

00:41:37.286 --> 00:41:40.331
And then as a community as a whole,

00:41:40.581 --> 00:41:46.045
they really try to protect
and they we treasure the kids.

00:42:15.324 --> 00:42:18.410
And one last person I'd like to introduce
is Jonathan Silverman.

00:42:18.661 --> 00:42:22.164
He's a ceramicist at Shelburne Craft
School in Vermont in the United States.

00:42:22.665 --> 00:42:24.041
He was visiting Nishiawakura

00:42:24.041 --> 00:42:27.294
to lead some art workshops and experience,
and a Noborigama firing.

00:42:27.378 --> 00:42:29.922
For those of you who've never 
heard of a Noborigama before,

00:42:29.922 --> 00:42:31.840
it's a type of ascending kiln.

00:42:32.466 --> 00:42:35.469
I have met people from all walks of life.

00:42:36.387 --> 00:42:38.847
Led a workshop for children

00:42:38.847 --> 00:42:41.934
and I’m going to be leading a workshop
for adults later on.

00:42:42.059 --> 00:42:46.021
And so in the short amount of time,
4 or 5 days,

00:42:46.855 --> 00:42:51.277
I really have a sense that this is,
Nishiawakura is a hidden gem.

00:42:51.569 --> 00:42:54.572
It's the nature of the people
that are here

00:42:54.738 --> 00:42:59.159
or who have somehow managed
to emigrate here,

00:42:59.577 --> 00:43:03.956
bringing in a lot of resources,
whether they lived in Tokyo or Okayama.

00:43:03.956 --> 00:43:08.794
They're bringing in some of the
the new ideas about technology,

00:43:08.794 --> 00:43:12.631
about sustainability,
artisan work and whatever it is.

00:43:12.631 --> 00:43:17.219
And I think that really allows
the community to thrive.

00:43:18.178 --> 00:43:20.598
Jonathan has successfully gotten
funding in America,

00:43:20.598 --> 00:43:24.602
to send several artists from Vermont
to do an artist residency in Nishiawakura.

00:43:24.977 --> 00:43:26.687
If the program is considered a success,

00:43:26.687 --> 00:43:29.857
then artists from Nishiawakura
may go to Vermont for their residencies.

00:43:30.274 --> 00:43:32.318
Hopefully,
this launches a beneficial long term

00:43:32.318 --> 00:43:35.279
relationship between the state of 
Vermont and Nishiawakura.

00:43:36.238 --> 00:43:38.574
One of the reasons
why I enjoyed visiting Nishiawakura

00:43:38.574 --> 00:43:41.368
is that many of the people
there have a sense of purpose.

00:43:41.368 --> 00:43:44.079
They're active and busy, 
but not so busyand frazzled 

00:43:44.079 --> 00:43:45.956
to not have a coffee with you.

00:43:45.956 --> 00:43:47.166
Or, in the case of this video,

00:43:47.166 --> 00:43:50.169
to be interviewed for a YouTube channel
that they'd never heard of.

00:43:50.419 --> 00:43:53.464
I learned so much about forestry,
biomass boilers, Kyoryokutai,

00:43:53.881 --> 00:43:57.301
and a whole laundry list of research
topics along the way to making this video.

00:43:57.551 --> 00:44:00.554
I'm proud that we were able to document
the works of these ambitious people,

00:44:00.763 --> 00:44:03.766
and I hope that people watching
this video may be inspired by them.

00:44:03.849 --> 00:44:07.061
Be ambitious, try to live a healthy life,
take care of your environment,

00:44:07.186 --> 00:44:10.022
and ultimately take care of your town.
Thanks for watching.

00:44:10.022 --> 00:44:11.774
See you next time. Peace.

00:44:12.107 --> 00:44:14.360
What are entrepreneurial villages
like where you're from?

